.TH MAPS-MQTT-MESH-TEST 1 "25 November 2026" "maps-apps 4.6.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
maps-mqtt-mesh-test \- replay MQTT logs and optionally verify delivery at multiple brokers
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B maps-mqtt-mesh-test
.BI --dir " directory"
.BI --broker " url"
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B maps-mqtt-mesh-test
replays recorded MQTT events from MAPS logger NDJSON files to a publishing broker.
The files may be plain
.B .ndjson
or gzip-compressed
.BR .ndjson.gz .
Files are discovered recursively and merged by recorded timestamp; each individual
file must already be in timestamp order. The application sends the reconstructed
message payload on its original topic.
.PP
Without any
.B --observe
arguments, the command only replays events. Each
.B --observe
argument adds an independent MQTT subscriber for delivery validation. All
subscriptions are established before replay begins. Subscribers may connect to
the publishing broker or to other brokers in a chain or mesh.
.PP
The tool uses MQTT 3.1.1 and does not attempt to discover topology or infer
a message's path through the brokers.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI --dir " directory"
Directory containing recorded .ndjson and .ndjson.gz files. Required.
.TP
.BI --broker " url"
MQTT publishing broker URL, for example tcp://127.0.0.1:1883. Required.
.TP
.BI --speed " factor"
Replay speed multiplier. Default: 1.0. Must be finite and greater than zero.
For example, 100 compresses recorded timestamp intervals by a factor of 100.
Actual throughput is limited by publishing and broker performance.
.TP
.B --no-delay
Publish events back-to-back without intentional timestamp waits.
If given with
.BR --speed ,
no-delay takes precedence. MQTT publishing and acknowledgements can still
limit actual throughput.
.TP
.B --session-per-topic
Use one MQTT publishing client session for each distinct full topic.
Mutually exclusive with
.BR --session-prefix .
.TP
.BI --session-prefix " path"
Use one publishing client session for each immediate child beneath the given
absolute MQTT topic path. For example, with /main/sub, the topics
/main/sub/unit1/position and /main/sub/unit1/status share a session,
while /main/sub/unit2/position uses another. Topics outside the prefix,
or without both a child folder and a topic below it, are rejected.
Mutually exclusive with
.BR --session-per-topic .
.TP
.BI --session-idle-ms " milliseconds"
Disconnect and close publishing sessions that have not published for the
configured interval. Default: 60000 ms. Must be positive. Closed sessions
are recreated on demand. The progress session count reflects open connections.
.TP
.BI --qos " 0|1|2"
Fallback publishing QoS when the recording has none. Default: 1.
Recorded QoS and retain values are used when available.
.TP
.BI --observe " url"
Subscribe to this MQTT broker and independently validate received events.
Repeat for any number of observation points. With no observation URLs,
validation is disabled and the command runs in replay-only mode.
.TP
.BI --observe-topic " filter"
MQTT subscription topic filter used on every observation point. Default: #.
Choose a filter covering all published topics to avoid false missing results.
.TP
.BI --drain-ms " milliseconds"
Continue collecting observations after the final publication for this number
of milliseconds. Default: 5000. Must be non-negative.
.TP
.BI --report " file"
Write a pretty-printed JSON validation report to the specified file in
addition to reporting on standard output. Used when observers are configured.
.SH VALIDATION
Each observation point is compared independently with the published events,
using the exact MQTT topic, a SHA-256 hash of the reconstructed payload bytes,
and occurrence counts. Arrival order is not assumed across topics.
.PP
The report includes published, received, matched, missing, excess, and unexpected
event counts, observer connection errors, and up to 100 mismatch examples per
observer. A validation failure results in a nonzero exit status.
.SH EXAMPLES
Replay the REPMUS archive at 100 times the recorded rate:
.PP
.EX
maps-mqtt-mesh-test --dir /data/repmus \
  --broker tcp://s1:1883 --speed 100
.EE
.PP
Replay to S1 and validate deliveries observed at S1, S2, and S3:
.PP
.EX
maps-mqtt-mesh-test --dir /data/repmus \
  --broker tcp://s1:1883 --speed 100 \
  --session-prefix /main_folder/sub_folder \
  --observe tcp://s1:1883 \
  --observe tcp://s2:1883 \
  --observe tcp://s3:1883 \
  --observe-topic '#' --drain-ms 15000 \
  --report results.json
.EE
.SH LIMITATIONS
Repeated identical messages on the same topic cannot be individually identified;
occurrence counts can conceal a loss offset by an identical duplicate.
Retained messages and unrelated traffic may appear as unexpected observations.
Large datasets with many distinct topic/payload combinations may require
substantial memory for validation. Messages arriving after the drain period
are counted as missing.
.PP
The logger JSON format cannot necessarily reconstruct original binary MQTT
payloads, JSON whitespace, MQTT 5 properties, or upstream client identifiers.
The tool does not check MAVLink command equivalence.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /opt/maps/apps/mqtt_mesh_test.jar
Executable JAR installed by the maps-apps Debian package.
